n  top it's cooler so it's more dense and  wants to go down but there was something  about the patterns on pluto's surface  that didn't quite fit with the idea of  convection currents being driven by  pluto's internal heat  eating from below would lead to actually  reversed patterns compared to what we  observe that is  flow going up on the polygons sides  instead of going down as we observe  the visible lines delineating the  polygons are troughs showing that the  convection currents must be rising up in  the center of each shape and sinking at  the edges the opposite to what you'd get  if you were heating from underneath so  we still think that convection is at the  origin of these polygons  but instead of eating from below  we think that it is better explained by  cooling the top to explain this cooling  stefan and his team turned to  sublimation  this is the process similar to  evaporation by which the nitrogen ice of  sputnik planitia turns from a solid  straight into a gas this cools down the  surface in a very similar way to the  situation we encounter in everyday life  when we get out of water and we feel  cold because water is evaporating which  requires latent heat to to do so and it  cools down the skin  and so we think that's exactly what's  happening to test this theory the team  developed a computer simulation of the  surface  and we found that with reasonable  parameters for sputnik planisia we  obtained the same type of patterns as  what we observe  their model shows for the first time  that sublimation can drive planetary  scale patterns  the team are confident in their findings  but it might be a while before all their  hypotheses can be fully tested it would  be good to go back there and test some  of the ideas that we propose but of  course this is very far away and it's  very difficult to send missions there  but i'm confident yes  you
 

